---
import ProjectLayout from "../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import Walkthrough from "../../components/Walkthrough.astro";
---
<ProjectLayout slug="alexanderk-dev" highlight>
  <Walkthrough title="How it’s built">
      <p class="section-intro">How this site works, from the files you see in the repo to the live GitHub numbers. You'll want to know basic HTML, CSS and JavaScript going in.</p>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>1. The big picture</h3>
        <p>What your browser downloads is <b>plain files</b>: HTML pages, one CSS file and a few JavaScript files. Most pages run no framework (like React) in the browser. The two exceptions are the most interactive ones, the <a href="/lab/chicago-home-value">Chicago home value map</a> and my private fitness dashboard: they're built from <b>Svelte</b> components that redraw themselves when the data changes, and only those pages load them.</p>
        <p>The pages are written with <b>Astro</b>, a static site generator. The header, footer and page shell live in one layout file, and each project and research post is an entry in a small data file. When the site is built, Astro stitches those together into one finished HTML file per page. So a change to the header is one edit, not twenty.</p>
        <p>The one exception is a small script that runs on Cloudflare and keeps the GitHub numbers up to date. Here's how a visit works:</p>
<pre is:raw><code>your browser ──asks for a page──► Cloudflare ──► the HTML, CSS and JS files
     │
     └─asks for /data/github.json─► Cloudflare script ──► saved GitHub data
                                         ▲
                       every 5 minutes: asks GitHub for fresh numbers</code></pre>

        <h3>2. The files</h3>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>src/layouts/</code></td><td>The page shell (head, header, footer) and the project and post layouts</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/pages/</code></td><td>One file per page: Home, About, Work, Research, Stats, plus the sitemap</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/data/</code></td><td>The list of projects and research posts that the pages and lists are built from</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/components/</code></td><td>Pieces shared by several pages, and the Svelte components behind the home value map and the fitness dashboard</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>src/styles/</code></td><td>The styling every page shares (<code>site.css</code>), and each page’s own layout</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>public/fonts/</code></td><td>The three typefaces, served from the site itself</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>public/js/gh.js</code>, <code>github.js</code>, <code>stats.js</code></td><td>Fill in the READMEs and the Stats page</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>worker/index.js</code></td><td>The Cloudflare script that fetches from GitHub</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>wrangler.jsonc</code>, <code>astro.config.mjs</code></td><td>Cloudflare and Astro settings</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>3. Styling</h3>
        <p>Every color and font is defined once, as a <b>CSS variable</b> at the top of <code>site.css</code>. The rest of the stylesheet only uses the variables, so changing a color is one edit:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-css">:root {
  --paper: #151412;    /* solid panels: charts, code, tables */
  --ink: #EDE9E1;      /* text */
  --accent: #6FD39A;   /* link underlines, labels, focus rings */
}

body { color: var(--ink); }</code></pre>
        <p>The site is dark only. Across the top of every page is Hubble&rsquo;s Ultra Deep Field, thousands of galaxies in one small patch of sky, fading into the dark page below. The photo is dimmed in the image file itself, text gets a soft dark edge so a bright galaxy never washes it out, and charts, maps, tables and code sit on solid panels.</p>
        <p>Headlines are set in Newsreader, text in Libre Franklin, and labels and dates in IBM Plex Mono. All three are open-source fonts served from the site itself, cut down to the Latin letters the site uses. Until they load, a font already on your device stands in, resized to take up the same space, so the text doesn't jump when they arrive.</p>

        <h3>4. Live GitHub numbers</h3>
        <p>The stars, commits and languages on the GitHub Stats page, and the READMEs on the project pages, come from GitHub. The obvious approach is to have each visitor's browser ask GitHub directly. The catch: GitHub only allows <b>60 requests an hour</b> per visitor without a key, and the Stats page alone needs dozens. Reload a few times and the numbers stop loading.</p>
        <p>The fix is to have <b>one</b> program ask GitHub on everyone's behalf and share the answer. That program is a Cloudflare <b>Worker</b>, a small script that runs on Cloudflare's servers. Cloudflare starts it every 5 minutes:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-json">// wrangler.jsonc
"triggers": { "crons": ["*/5 * * * *"] }</code></pre>
        <p>Each run, the Worker:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Asks GitHub for every repo's stars, commits, languages and last update, all in one request (a GraphQL query).</li>
          <li>Gets recent activity, plus any README that changed since last time.</li>
          <li>Saves everything as one JSON file in Cloudflare's storage (KV), but only if something changed.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>When a browser asks for <code>/data/github.json</code>, the Worker hands back that saved file. Every other address is a normal file:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-js">async fetch(request, env) {
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  // any other address: a normal file
  if (url.pathname !== "/data/github.json") return env.ASSETS.fetch(request);
  // the data file: the saved GitHub data
  const body = await env.GITHUB_DATA.get("github.json");
  return new Response(body, { headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" } });
}</code></pre>
        <p>On a project page, the folded README at the bottom says which repo it wants:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-html">&lt;div data-readme="amkashyap1220/SwitchToYoutube"&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>The first time you open it, <code>github.js</code> looks the repo up in the data file and fills it in. Until then the page doesn't download the data at all:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-js">fold.addEventListener("toggle", () =&gt; {
  readme(box.dataset.readme).then((html) =&gt; {
    box.innerHTML = html;   // already made safe by GitHub
  });
});</code></pre>
        <p>Now visitors' browsers never talk to GitHub at all, and the numbers and READMEs are never more than about 5 minutes old.</p>

        <h3>5. The Stats page is a small data pipeline</h3>
        <p><code>stats.js</code> is organized like an <b>ETL</b> job, the extract-transform-load pattern data engineers use everywhere:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Extract:</b> read the repos, languages and activity from the data file.</li>
          <li><b>Transform:</b> add up totals, like bytes of code per language across every repo, or the busiest weekday.</li>
          <li><b>Load:</b> draw the results: the number tiles, the language bars and the activity summary.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For example, here's the transform step that totals up languages:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-js">function languageTotals(repos) {
  const totals = {};
  repos.forEach((r) =&gt; {
    Object.entries(r.languages).forEach(([lang, bytes]) =&gt; {
      totals[lang] = totals[lang] || { name: lang, bytes: 0, repos: 0 };
      totals[lang].bytes += bytes;    // how much code
      totals[lang].repos += 1;        // how many repos use it
    });
  });
  return Object.values(totals);
}</code></pre>

        <h3>6. Shipping changes</h3>
        <p>The repo is connected to Cloudflare, so <b>every push to <code>main</code> deploys the site</b> automatically. Cloudflare runs <code>npx wrangler deploy</code>, which first runs the tests (they build the site and check every page and link), then builds the pages with Astro and uploads them along with the Worker. If a test fails, nothing is deployed. It's live in under a minute.</p>
        <p>Changes don't go straight to <code>main</code>, though. Each one goes on its own branch with a pull request. Cloudflare builds a preview of the branch, and it gets merged once it looks right:</p>
<pre is:raw><code class="language-bash">git checkout -b add-new-project     # new branch
# ...make changes...
git push -u origin add-new-project  # Cloudflare builds a preview
# open a pull request, check the preview, merge → live</code></pre>
      </div>
  </Walkthrough>
</ProjectLayout>
